    <DataNode elementId="CPD-1142" textLabel="salicin" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="NGFMICBWJRZIBI-UJPOAAIJSA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, SRI, caspi">|FRAME: CPD-1142 Salicin| is a phenolic glycoside (PG) that makes an important contribution to plant defense against herbivores in |FRAME: TAX-3689| and |FRAME: TAX-40685| species |CITS: [20177744][11504029]|. It is the most prevalent salicylate compound in certain northern willows |CITS: [12921436]|. Studies related to this compound have been undertaken in a number of species including |FRAME: TAX-339964|, |FRAME: TAX-77065|, |FRAME: TAX-75715|, and |FRAME: TAX-3691| |CITS: [12921436][ZENK67][20177744]|. In general, a number of different phenolic glycosides, including |FRAME: CPD-13173| and |FRAME: CPD-1142| can accumulate to extremely high levels in leaves of |FRAME: TAX-3689| and |FRAME: TAX-40685| species, with exact levels of specific PGs varying based on genotype, environment, time of day, and developmental stage |CITS: [17226439][11504029][16724272][5886251]|. Interestingly, there is evidence that the creation of large amounts of these compounds may impose a metabolic cost that can negatively impact plant growth |CITS: [11504029][16411958]|, suggesting that plants must finely tune PG biosynthesis to maximize overall fitness. |FRAME: CPD-1142 Salicin| may deter herbivore attack when it is hydrolyzed by &amp;beta;-glucosidases to |FRAME: CPD-173| in insect guts, possibly leading to the formation of gut lesions |CITS: [12921436]|. The common drug |FRAME: CPD-524| has been generated by a chemical modification of |FRAME: CPD-1142|.</Comment>
